{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"Given that \\( z \\) is a complex number such that \\( z^{13} = 1 \\), \\( z \\) is a 13th root of unity. Therefore, \\( z \\) can be written as \\( z = e^{2\\pi i k / 13} \\) for \\( k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 12 \\).\n\nWe need to find the sum of the squares of all possible values of the expression \\( z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12} \\).\n\nFirst, observe that \\( z^k \\) for \\( k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 12 \\) are all the 13th roots of unity. The sum of all 13th roots of unity is zero:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{12} z^j = 0.\n\\]\n\nTo find the sum of the squares of all possible values of \\( z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12} \\), we need to consider the polynomial whose roots are these values. Let \\( P(x) = (x - (z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12}))(x - (z' + z'^3 + z'^4 + z'^9 + z'^{10} + z'^{12})) \\cdots \\).\n\nThe polynomial whose roots are \\( z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12} \\) for all \\( z \\) that are 13th roots of unity can be derived from the polynomial whose roots are the 13th roots of unity. The polynomial is:\n\\[\nx^{13} - 1 = (x - 1)(x^{12} + x^{11} + \\cdots + x + 1).\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1068,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[22043,429,17767,1147,1124,8,374,264,6351,1372,1741,429,17767,1147,47822,16,18,92,284,220,16,1124,701,17767,1147,1124,8,374,264,220,16,18,339,3704,315,30326,13,15277,11,17767,1147,1124,8,646,387,5326,438,17767,1147,284,384,47822,17,59,2493,600,595,608,220,16,18,92,1124,8,369,17767,595,284,220,15,11,220,16,11,220,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,220,16,17,1124,3593,1654,1184,311,1477,279,2629,315,279,31340,315,678,3204,2750,315,279,7493,17767,1147,488,1147,61,18,488,1147,61,19,488,1147,61,24,488,1147,47822,16,15,92,488,1147,47822,16,17,92,1124,3593,5338,11,22986,429,17767,1147,61,74,1124,8,369,17767,595,284,220,15,11,220,16,11,220,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,220,16,17,1124,8,52