0048828,-0.029249245300889015,-2.264974000354414e-06,-0.002709529362618923,-3.3378546504536644e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,400,89,3,387,264,6351,1372,1741,429,400,89,47822,16,18,92,284,220,16,2418,220,6771,400,86,62,16,4779,400,86,62,17,4779,57960,67816,4779,400,86,4698,3,387,678,279,3204,2750,315,198,78045,89,488,1147,61,18,488,1147,61,19,488,1147,61,24,488,1147,47822,16,15,92,488,1147,47822,16,17,92,7110,60,9885,400,86,62,16,61,17,488,289,62,17,61,17,488,1124,67816,488,289,4698,61,17,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $z$ be a complex number such that $z^{13} = 1.$  Let $w_1,$ $w_2,$ $\\dots,$ $w_k$ be all the possible values of\n\\[z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12}.\\]Find $w_1^2 + w_2^2 + \\dots + w_k^2.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"650243ec544a031ade9d40e7da94ac928a3ce47d96d0931ce78461fdbcc14701"},"seconds":18.236361265182495,"seed":165288491325980144,"stop":"eos","text":"Given that \\( z \\) is a complex number such that \\( z^{13} = 1 \\), \\( z \\) is a 13th root of unity. Therefore, \\( z \\) can be written as \\( z = e^{2\\pi i k / 13} \\) for \\( k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 12 \\).\n\nWe need to find the sum of the squares of all possible values of the expression \\( z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12} \\).\n\nFirst, observe that \\( z^k \\) for \\( k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 12 \\) are all the 13th roots of unity. The sum of all 13th roots of unity is zero:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{12} z^j = 0.\n\\]\n\nTo find the sum of the squares of all possible values of \\( z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12} \\), we need to consider the polynomial whose roots are these values. Let \\( P(x) = (x - (z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12}))(x - (z' + z'^3 + z'^4 + z'^9 + z'^{10} + z'^{12})) \\cdots \\).\n\nThe polynomial whose roots are \\( z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12} \\) for all \\( z \\) that are 13th roots of unity can be derived from the polynomial whose roots are the 13th roots of unity. The polynomial is:\n\\[\nx^{13} - 1 = (x - 1)(x^{12} + x^{11} + \\cdots + x + 1).\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the sum of the squares of the roots of the polynomial formed by the sums \\( z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12} \\). This can be done using the identity for the sum of the squares of the roots of a polynomial. If \\( \\alpha_1, \\alpha_2, \\ldots, \\alpha_k \\) are the roots of a polynomial \\( P(x) \\), then:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^k \\alpha_i^2 = \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^k \\alpha_i \\right)^2 - 2 \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq k} \\alpha_i \\alpha_j.\n\\]\n\nIn our case, the polynomial is derived from the roots of unity, and the sum of all possible values of \\( z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12} \\) can be found by considering the symmetry and properties of the roots of unity.\n\nThe sum of the squares of all possible values of \\( z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12} \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:346"}
